The '''2022 Westminster City Council election''' took place on 5 May 2022. All 54 members of [[Westminster City Council]] were elected. The elections took place alongside [[2022 London local elections|local elections in the other London boroughs]] and [[2022 United Kingdom local elections|elections to local authorities across the United Kingdom]].

In the [[2018 Westminster City Council election|previous election in 2018]], the [[Conservative Party (UK)|Conservative Party]] had maintained their longstanding control of the council, winning 41 out of the 60 seats with the [[Labour Party (UK)|Labour Party]] forming the council opposition with the remaining 19 seats. However, Labour won an 8-seat council majority for the first time since the [[London Government Act 1963|formation of the modern city]] in 1964.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Neary |first1=Hannah |title=Tories lose Westminster Council to Labour for first time ever |url=https://www.mylondon.news/news/west-london-news/westminster-local-election-results-2022-23879454 |work=MyLondon |date=6 May 2022 |language=en}}</ref> The 2022 election took place under new election boundaries, reducing the number of councillors to 54.

== Background ==

=== History ===
[[File:Westminster London UK local election 2018 map.svg|thumb|Result of the 2018 borough election]]
The thirty-two [[London boroughs]] were established in 1965 by the [[London Government Act 1963]]. They are the principal authorities in Greater London and have responsibilities including education, housing, planning, highways, social services, libraries, recreation, waste, environmental health and revenue collection. Some of the powers are shared with the [[Greater London Authority]], which also manages passenger transport, police, and fire.<ref>{{Cite web|title=The essential guide to London local government {{!}} London Councils|url=https://www.londoncouncils.gov.uk/who-runs-london/essential-guide-london-local-government|access-date=2021-10-10|website=www.londoncouncils.gov.uk}}</ref>

Westminster City Council had continuously been under [[Conservative Party (UK)|Conservative Party]] control since its establishment. In the [[2018 Westminster City Council election|most recent election in 2018]], Westminster was considered a key target for Labour in London.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Hill|first=Dave|date=2018-04-27|title=Westminster 2018: Labour looks for a big swing in Little Venice|url=https://www.onlondon.co.uk/westminster-2018-labour-looks-for-a-big-swing-in-little-venice/|access-date=2021-10-22|website=OnLondon|language=en-GB}}</ref> The Conservatives won the election, with 41 seats on 42.8% of the vote across the borough, while Labour won 19 seats, with 41.1% of the vote.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Gregory|first=Julia|date=2018-05-04|title=Westminster local election results: Conservatives retain control|url=https://www.getwestlondon.co.uk/news/west-london-news/westminster-local-election-results-conservatives-14613637|access-date=2021-10-22|website=[[MyLondon]]|language=en}}</ref>

=== Council term ===

The Conservative councillor [[Robert Davis (British politician)|Robert Davis]], who represented Lancaster Gate and had served on the council since 1982, resigned after an investigation into his conduct found that he broke the councillors' code of conduct for receiving a large number of gifts and hospitality from property developers.<ref>{{Cite news|date=2018-10-10|title=Westminster councillor Robert Davis resigns over conduct investigation|language=en-GB|work=BBC News|url=https://www.bbc.com/news/uk-england-london-45815856|access-date=2021-10-22}}</ref> The 22 November 2018 by-election was held for the Conservatives by Margot Bright with the Labour candidate coming in second place.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Baston|first=Lewis|date=2018-11-23|title=Westminster: Tory by-election hold in Lancaster Gate is more significant than it appears|url=https://www.onlondon.co.uk/westminster-tory-by-election-hold-in-lancaster-gate-is-more-significant-than-it-appears/|access-date=2021-10-22|website=OnLondon|language=en-GB}}</ref> In March 2021, Andrea Mann, a Labour councillor for Churchill ward, resigned for family reasons.<ref>{{Cite web|date=2021-03-15|title=Andrea Mann steps down as councillor for Churchill Ward - Westminster Labour Councillors|url=https://www.westminsterlabour.org.uk/ward-news/2021/03/15/andrea-mann-steps-down-as-councillor-for-churchill-ward/|access-date=2021-10-22|website=Westminster Labour|language=en-GB}}</ref> The by-election to fill the seat was held on 6 May 2021 alongside the [[2021 London mayoral election]] and [[2021 London Assembly election|London Assembly election]]. The Labour candidate Liza Begum won, with an increased majority compared to the 2018 election.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Sheppard|first=Owen|date=2021-05-07|title=Labour wins by-election in Tory stronghold Westminster Council|url=https://www.mylondon.news/news/zone-1-news/labours-liza-begum-wins-churchill-20550929|access-date=2021-10-22|website=MyLondon|language=en}}</ref>

Along with most London boroughs, this election occurred under new ward boundaries. Following local consultation, the [[Local Government Boundary Commission for England]] produced new boundaries, reducing the number of councillors from 60 to 54 across eighteen three-councillor wards.<ref>{{Cite web |last=LGBCE |title=Westminster {{!}} LGBCE |url=https://www.lgbce.org.uk/all-reviews/westminster |access-date=2026-04-13 |website=www.lgbce.org.uk |language=en}}</ref>

== Campaign ==
The Conservative peer [[Robert Hayward, Baron Hayward|Robert Hayward]] listed Westminster as one of four Conservative councils in London that his party risked losing control of in the wake of the [[partygate]] scandal. The concentration of Labour voters in a small number of wards means that relatively few seats in the borough are marginal.<ref name=":0">{{Cite web|last=Hill|first=Dave|date=2022-01-10|title=Borough elections 2022: Will London sink Boris Johnson in May?|url=https://www.onlondon.co.uk/borough-elections-2022-will-london-sink-boris-johnson-in-may/|access-date=2022-02-16|website=OnLondon|language=en-GB}}</ref> Labour criticised the Conservative council for the [[Marble Arch Mound]]'s cost, which had led to the resignation of the council's deputy leader [[Melvyn Caplan]].<ref name=":0" /><ref>{{Cite news|date=2021-08-13|title=Marble Arch Mound: Deputy leader resigns amid spiralling costs|language=en-GB|work=BBC News|url=https://www.bbc.com/news/uk-england-london-58197626|access-date=2022-02-16}}</ref> ''[[Fitzrovia News]]'' wrote that the new West End ward would be competitive between Labour and the Conservatives, with Labour having won one of the three seats on previous boundaries.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2022-04-02 |title=Local elections will be a close-run battle... on one side of Fitzrovia |url=http://fitzrovianews.com/2022/04/02/local-elections-will-be-a-close-run-battle-on-one-side-of-fitzrovia/ |access-date=2022-04-02 |website=Fitzrovia News |language=en-GB}}</ref>

== Electoral process ==
Westminster, as with all other London borough councils, elects all of its councillors at once every four years, with the previous election having taken place in 2018. At the same time elections were held for the 12 seats of [https://queensparkcommunitycouncil.gov.uk/ Queen's Park Community Council], the [[Parish council (England)|Parish council]] in the north west of the city, with these being administered by the [[Returning officer|Returning Officer]] at the City Council and both elections were counted together overnight.

The elections took place by multi-member [[first-past-the-post voting]], with each ward being represented by three councillors. Electors had as many votes as there are councillors to be elected in their ward, with the top three being elected.

All [[Elections in the United Kingdom#Registration procedure|registered electors]] ([[British citizen|British]], [[Irish citizen|Irish]], [[Commonwealth citizen|Commonwealth]] and [[European Union]] citizens) living in London aged 18 or over were entitled to vote in the election. People who lived at two addresses in different councils, such as university students with different term-time and holiday addresses, were entitled to be registered for and vote in elections in both local authorities.<ref name=":1">{{Cite web|title=How the elections work {{!}} London Councils|url=https://www.londoncouncils.gov.uk/who-runs-london/london-elections/how-elections-work|access-date=2021-09-14|website=www.londoncouncils.gov.uk}}</ref> Voting in-person at polling stations took place from 7:00 to 22:00 on election day, and voters were able to apply for postal votes or proxy votes in advance of the election.<ref name=":1" />

